\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Boundary conditions.}
The two-dimensional decomposition applies to cross-domain schemas; same-domain schemas exhibit centrality confounding (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). The effect generalizes to document-level RE (DocRED slope $-$0.71, adjusted $R^2 = 0.51$, $n = 35$; 95\% CI [0.27, 0.73]) and persists across prompt designs (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompt_sensitivity}). Dimension~1 replicates across scales (8B--70B); Dimension~2 attenuates on smaller models.

\paragraph{Why does embedding similarity predict LLM confusion?}
SCS captures semantic proximity in LLMs' type-resolution space. The unconditional pairwise $R^2$ is modest (1.3\%); conditional on nonzero confusion (${\sim}11\%$ of pairs), $R^2$ rises to 7\%. Correlated aggregation amplifies this to batch-level $R^2$ of 18--25\% (Monte Carlo simulation with parameters derived from the observed data; this simulation serves as an internal consistency check rather than independent evidence; Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation-sim}), consistent with observed RE values.

\paragraph{Task-dependent effects and format dependency.}
The batch-level BCS--performance relationship is RE-specific ($R^2 = 0.22$--$0.53$ vs.\ $r \approx 0$ for NER, $n = 450$). RE uses a forced-choice classification format where each instance maps to exactly one relation type; all non-null errors are necessarily type substitutions \cite{tam-2024-let-me-speak}, which makes BCS predictive. NER, despite also using constrained decoding on the type enum, produces multi-span output where errors scatter across missed entities, boundary misalignment, and type confusion, which dilutes the BCS signal. This format asymmetry is a boundary condition for our batch-level findings \cite{schall-2025-hidden-cost}: BCS predicts accuracy when task format channels errors into type substitution, but loses signal when errors distribute across multiple failure modes. We hypothesize the NER U-shape (\S\ref{sec:count_curves}) reflects a dual-task burden: partial schemas require simultaneous span detection and type-membership filtering; at full schema, filtering becomes vacuous and recall recovers. An unconstrained decoding ablation ($n = 25$ batches, removing the JSON enum constraint) reveals that the BCS--accuracy correlation largely disappears ($\rho = -0.14$, $p = 0.49$, $\Delta = +2.8$\,pp, 95\% CI [$-4.5$, $+10.6$]), while OOV remains near zero (0.08\%). This confirms that constrained decoding channels errors exclusively into type substitution, which amplifies the confusability signal; without this channeling, errors disperse across output modes and BCS loses batch-level predictive power.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
For RE, pre-screening type pairs in the upper SCS quartile identifies confusability risk (3--5\,pp expected impact); targeted few-shot examples may partially offset confusion but do not eliminate the gap. For NER, reducing candidate types per prompt is the primary lever, as confusability-aware batching provides no additional benefit. Schema partitioning remains non-viable due to merge amplification (NER) and routing uncertainty (RE). These are diagnostic guidelines derived from correlational evidence; practitioners should validate on their specific schema.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Schema-associated degradation in training-free LLM extraction operates along two largely additive dimensions: count-related recall decline and confusability-related type substitution, with bounded interaction in RE (under forced-choice classification) but inconclusive separation in NER. Count decline replicates across scales (8B--70B) and families including Gemini 2.5 Flash; confusability effects are structural (+4.3\,pp under 5-shot). Schema decomposition fails due to merge amplification (NER) and routing failure (RE). Future work should extend to fine-tuned models and merge-aware partitioning.

